          "name": "63-rhyme-tools",
          "sectionId": "1D9D3C55-2A6A-4AF7-AFC3-F81876599A63",
          "userOrder": 2,
          "versions": [
            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Rhyme Tools\n\nRhyme is a fundamental element of many poetry forms. Writing Shed Pro provides tools to help you identify, plan, and execute rhyme schemes.\n\n## Rhyme Scheme Display\n\nWhen working with a form that has a specific rhyme scheme:\n\n### Viewing the Scheme\n- The expected rhyme scheme appears in the form reference panel\n- Each line is labeled (A, B, C, etc.)\n- Same letters should rhyme\n\n### Example: Shakespearean Sonnet\n```\nLine 1  (A)    When in disgrace with fortune and men's eyes\nLine 2  (B)    I all alone beweep my outcast state\nLine 3  (A)    And trouble deaf heaven with my bootless cries\nLine 4  (B)    And look upon myself and curse my fate\n...\nLine 13 (G)    For thy sweet love remembered such wealth brings\nLine 14 (G)    That then I scorn to change my state with kings\n```\n\n## Types of Rhyme\n\nWriting Shed Pro recognizes various rhyme types:\n\n### Perfect Rhyme\nWords with identical endings:\n- cat/hat\n- love/dove\n- night/light\n\n### Near Rhyme (Slant Rhyme)\nWords with similar but not identical sounds:\n- love/move\n- eyes/light\n- moon/on\n\n### Internal Rhyme\nRhymes within a line:\n- \"Once upon a midnight dreary, while I pondered, weak and weary\"\n\n## Finding Rhymes\n\nWhen you need rhyming words:\n\n### Using Look Up\n1. Select a word you need to rhyme with\n2. Use the system Look Up feature\n3. Explore dictionary and thesaurus options\n\n### Manual Approach\nConsider:\n- Perfect rhymes first\n- Slant rhymes for more options\n- Near rhymes for subtle effects\n\n## Rhyme Scheme Tips\n\n### Plan Ahead\nFor complex schemes, sketch rhymes before writing:\n1. Identify your end words for A lines\n2. List possible B rhymes\n3. Continue for each rhyme group\n\n### Avoid Forced Rhyme\n- Don't sacrifice meaning for rhyme\n- Slant rhymes are often better than awkward perfect rhymes\n- The best rhyme feels inevitable, not forced\n\n### Consider End Sounds\nSome sounds offer more rhymes:\n- \"ay\" sounds (day, way, say, play...)\n- \"ight\" sounds (night, light, bright...)\n\nDifficult rhyming sounds:\n- \"orange\" (few true rhymes)\n- \"month\" (limited options)\n- \"silver\" (challenging)\n\n## Rhyme in Different Forms\n\n### Sonnets\n- Complex schemes require planning\n- 3 quatrains + couplet (Shakespearean)\n- Octave + sestet (Petrarchan)\n\n### Villanelle\n- Only two rhyme sounds (A and B)\n- Repeated lines add structure\n- Plan carefully before writing\n\n### Limerick\n- AABBA scheme\n- A lines typically longer\n- B lines short and punchy\n\n### Free Verse\n- No required rhyme\n- Use rhyme for effect when desired\n- Internal rhyme creates subtle music\n\n## Common Rhyming Challenges\n\n### Overused Rhymes\nAvoid clichéd pairs:\n- love/dove/above\n- heart/apart\n- moon/June\n\n### Inverted Syntax\nDon't twist sentences for rhyme:\n- \"The house so white\" ← awkward\n- \"The white house\" ← natural\n\n### Rhyme Fatigue\nIn long poems, vary:\n- Use slant rhymes occasionally\n- Place rhymes at unexpected spots\n- Give readers' ears a rest\n\n## Building Your Rhyme Vocabulary\n\n### Read Poetry\nExposure builds your mental rhyme dictionary.\n\n### Keep Lists\nNote good rhymes you discover.\n\n### Practice\nWrite exercises focusing on specific sounds.\n\n### Thesaurus\nFind synonyms that offer better rhymes.\n\n## See Also\n- [Poetry Mode Overview](01-poetry-mode-overview.md)\n- [Syllable Counting](02-syllable-counting.md)\n- [Forms and Templates](05-forms-and-templates.md)\n\n---\n",
