            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Chapter Management\n\nIn Novel projects, chapters organize your scenes into a reading order. This guide covers creating, organizing, and working with chapters.\n\n## Understanding Chapters\n\nChapters in Writing Shed Pro:\n- Are containers for scenes\n- Determine the order scenes appear in the manuscript\n- Can include multiple scenes\n- Live in the Chapters folder\n\n## Creating Chapters\n\n### From the Chapters Folder\n1. Open the **Chapters** folder\n2. Tap **+**\n3. Enter a chapter title (e.g., \"Chapter 1: The Beginning\")\n4. Tap **Create**\n\n### When to Create Chapters\n- After writing several scenes, or\n- Before writing (if you outline), or\n- During revision as structure emerges\n\nThere's no wrong time—do what fits your process.\n\n## Adding Scenes to Chapters\n\n### From the Scene\n1. Open a scene in Draft or Ready\n2. Access scene settings\n3. Select **Add to Chapter**\n4. Choose the chapter\n\n### From the Chapter\n1. Open a chapter\n2. Tap **Add Scene**\n3. Select from available scenes\n\n### Drag and Drop\nOn iPad/Mac:\n- Drag scenes from Draft/Ready\n- Drop onto a chapter\n- Scene is added to that chapter\n\n## Scene Order Within Chapters\n\nScenes appear in the chapter in a specific order:\n\n### Reordering Scenes\n1. Open the chapter\n2. Tap **Edit**\n3. Drag scenes using the handles\n4. Tap **Done**\n\nThe order determines how scenes appear in the assembled manuscript.\n\n## Renaming Chapters\n\n1. Open the Chapters folder\n2. Long-press the chapter\n3. Select **Rename**\n4. Enter the new title\n5. Tap **Done**\n\n## Deleting Chapters\n\n1. Swipe left on the chapter\n2. Tap **Delete**\n3. Confirm\n\n**Note**: Deleting a chapter does not delete the scenes—they remain in Draft/Ready. Only the chapter container is removed.\n\n## Chapter Order\n\nChapters appear in your manuscript in a specific order:\n\n### Reordering Chapters\n1. Open the Chapters folder\n2. Tap **Edit**\n3. Drag chapters using the handles\n4. Tap **Done**\n\n## Chapter Best Practices\n\n### Naming Conventions\nSome options:\n- \"Chapter 1\", \"Chapter 2\"... (simple)\n- \"Chapter 1: The Storm\" (numbered with title)\n- \"The Storm\" (title only)\n- \"1\" (minimalist)\n\nChoose a style and be consistent.\n\n### Chapter Length\nThere's no rule, but consider:\n- Reader attention spans\n- Natural story breaks\n- Pacing and rhythm\n\nShort chapters create urgency; long chapters allow immersion.\n\n### When Scenes Belong to No Chapter\nScenes don't have to be in chapters:\n- Keep works-in-progress in Draft\n- Use chapters only for manuscript organization\n- It's okay to have \"loose\" scenes\n\n### Multi-Chapter Scenes\nA scene can only belong to one chapter. If content spans chapters:\n- Split into multiple scenes\n- Or place in the chapter where it primarily belongs\n\n## Viewing Chapter Contents\n\nWhen you open a chapter:\n- See all scenes in order\n- View word counts\n- Access each scene for editing\n\n## Chapters and Manuscript\n\nWhen you assemble or export your manuscript:\n- Chapters appear in order\n- Scenes within chapters appear in order\n- Chapter titles become headings\n\nSee [Manuscript Formatting](04-manuscript-formatting.md) for export details.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### Scene Missing from Chapter\n- Check if it was removed\n- Look in Draft/Ready folders\n- Scenes must be added explicitly\n\n### Wrong Scene Order\n- Open chapter in Edit mode\n- Drag to correct order\n\n### Chapter Won't Delete\n- Ensure you have access\n- Check for app issues (restart if needed)\n\n## See Also\n- [Fiction Mode Overview](01-fiction-mode-overview.md)\n- [Scene Organization](03-scene-organization.md)\n- [Manuscript Formatting](04-manuscript-formatting.md)\n\n---\n",
              "createdDate": "2026-02-03T15:02:43Z",
