        {
          "contentTypeRaw": "markdown",
          "createdDate": "2026-02-03T15:33:11Z",
          "currentVersionIndex": 0,
          "id": "44EFFE14-6CB0-4313-98B3-EE59027A741E",
          "includedInManuscript": true,
          "modifiedDate": "2026-02-03T15:35:27Z",
          "name": "106-tips-and-tricks",
          "sectionId": "1A4A60D7-55FE-49AF-9636-75122540D6B9",
          "userOrder": 5,
          "versions": [
            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Tips and Tricks\n\nPower tips to get the most out of Writing Shed Pro.\n\n## Writing Tips\n\n### Write First, Format Later\nDon't get distracted by fonts and styles while drafting. Get your ideas down first, then polish.\n\n### Use the Right Project Type\nChoose Poetry, Fiction, or Drama when appropriate. The specialized tools are worth it.\n\n### Let Auto-Save Work\nStop hitting Cmd+S. Your work saves constantly. Trust the system.\n\n### Read Aloud\nEven with syllable counting and stress analysis, nothing replaces reading your work aloud.\n\n## Organization Tips\n\n### Use Collections, Not Copies\nInstead of duplicating files for different purposes, add them to multiple collections. One source of truth.\n\n### Move to Ready When Done\nMoving files to Ready isn't just organization—it's a psychological commitment that the piece is finished.\n\n### Empty Trash Regularly\nOld deleted files take up space and clutter your backups. Clean up monthly.\n\n### Use Subfolders Sparingly\nTwo or three levels deep is plenty. Too much nesting makes things hard to find.\n\n## Editing Tips\n\n### Use Find and Replace Carefully\nAlways preview before Replace All. It's faster to check twice than to undo a mess.\n\n### Master the Selection Menu\nDouble-tap for word, triple-tap for paragraph. The selection menu offers powerful options.\n\n### Learn Five Shortcuts\nYou don't need to learn them all. Start with: ⌘Z, ⌘B, ⌘I, ⌘F, and ⌘C/V. Add more as needed.\n\n### Comments for Future You\nLeave comments on tricky passages: \"Check this date\" or \"Consider cutting this scene.\"\n\n## Poetry Tips\n\n### Trust Your Ear\nThe syllable counter and stress analyzer are guides, not gods. If your ear says it works, it works.\n\n### Try Templates\nEven if you write free verse, try a sonnet or villanelle sometime. Constraints spark creativity.\n\n### One Poem Per File\nIt's tempting to put all your haiku in one file, but individual files are easier to organize and submit.\n\n## Fiction Tips\n\n### Scenes Are Small\nA scene should be one continuous action. If in doubt, make it smaller.\n\n### Link to Plot Elements\nConnecting scenes to plot elements helps you see your story's structure. Do it even if you're a \"pantser.\"\n\n### Use Character and Location Notes\nYou'll forget details six months into a novel. Record them now.\n\n### Don't Over-Plan\nThe tools for chapters and plot elements are there if you want them. You don't have to use everything.\n\n## Drama Tips\n\n### Learn Basic DML\nJust the essentials: `#` for headings, `>` for action, character names in caps. That's 80% of what you need.\n\n### Check Both Formats\nToggle between Film and Stage output to see how your script adapts. It might reveal opportunities.\n\n### Read Scripts\nStudy professional scripts in your target format. Internalize the conventions.\n\n## Export Tips\n\n### Preview in Pagination View\nAlways check pagination before export. Catch issues early.\n\n### PDF for Submissions\nUnless specifically asked for something else, PDF is the safest choice.\n\n### Match the Requirements\nRead submission guidelines carefully. Format exactly as requested.\n\n## Sync Tips\n\n### Trust iCloud\nFiles sync automatically. No need to force anything.\n\n### Wait Before Switching Devices\nIf you've just made changes, wait a few seconds for sync before opening on another device.\n\n### Keep Backups\nExport important finished work as PDFs. It's a good safety net.\n\n## Hidden Features\n\n### Long-Press Back Button\nJump directly to the project root from any nested view.\n\n### Pinch to Zoom\nIn the editor, pinch to zoom for comfortable reading or precise editing.\n\n### Shake to Undo\nOn iPhone, shake the device to trigger undo.\n\n### Three-Finger Swipe\nLeft for undo, right for redo. Works in most text areas.\n\n## Workflow Suggestions\n\n### Daily Writing\n1. Open project\n2. Create new file in Draft\n3. Write without editing\n4. Save (automatic)\n5. Come back tomorrow\n\n### Revision Session\n1. Open file from Draft\n2. Read through\n3. Use comments to mark issues\n4. Make changes\n5. When satisfied, move to Ready\n\n### Submission Day\n1. Review files in Ready\n2. Create Collection for submission\n3. Export or submit\n4. Record the submission\n5. Track status\n\n## Getting Help\n\n### Check This Guide\nMost questions are answered here. Use search (⌘F) to find specific topics.\n\n### Restart If Stuck\nIf something seems broken, try force-quitting and reopening the app.\n\n### Report Bugs\nFound a real bug? Report it through the app or support channels.\n\n## See Also\n- [Keyboard Shortcuts](05-keyboard-shortcuts.md)\n- [Troubleshooting](../11-reference/03-troubleshooting.md)\n- [FAQ](../11-reference/04-faq.md)\n\n---\n",
