          "contentTypeRaw": "markdown",
          "createdDate": "2026-02-03T15:03:56Z",
          "currentVersionIndex": 0,
          "id": "3B5FE2F5-F5BF-47CA-BBE4-2E722027CDBD",
          "includedInManuscript": true,
          "modifiedDate": "2026-02-11T12:00:00Z",
          "name": "74-manuscript-formatting",
          "sectionId": "9024754E-EEB6-4B61-8390-BF5FD3CA8E54",
          "userOrder": 3,
          "versions": [
            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Manuscript Formatting (Fiction)\n\nThis guide covers how Writing Shed Pro assembles fiction project content into a manuscript for export. Each fiction class has its own assembly model. For general manuscript structure (front matter, back matter), see [Manuscript Structure](../3-projects/36-manuscript-structure.md).\n\n## How Fiction Manuscripts Are Assembled\n\n### Novel\n1. Chapters are assembled in chapter order\n2. Scenes within each chapter are combined in scene order\n3. Chapter headings are inserted between chapters\n4. The result is a single, continuous manuscript\n\n### Verse Novel\n1. Books are assembled in book order\n2. Episodes within each book are combined in episode order\n3. Poetry formatting (line breaks, stanza breaks, indentation) is preserved\n4. The result is a continuous verse manuscript\n\n### Short Fiction\n1. Scenes are assembled in scene order\n2. Scene breaks are inserted between scenes\n3. The result is a complete story\n\n### What Gets Included\n\nBy default, all scenes are included. You can exclude individual scenes:\n1. Open the scene's context menu\n2. Toggle **Include in Manuscript** off\n3. The scene stays in your project but is skipped during assembly\n\nThis is useful for deleted scenes you want to keep, alternate versions, or notes.\n\n## Previewing the Manuscript\n\nBefore exporting:\n1. Open the **Manuscript** view\n2. Check chapter/book breaks appear correctly\n3. Verify scene order within each chapter\n4. Review formatting\n5. Confirm no unwanted scenes are included\n\n## Chapter Headings\n\nControl how chapter headings appear in the manuscript:\n\n| Option | Example Output |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Number only** | Chapter 1 |\n| **Title only** | The Storm |\n| **Number and title** | Chapter 1: The Storm |\n| **Custom format** | I — The Storm |\n\n### Setting Chapter Headings\n1. Open **Export Options** or **Project Settings**\n2. Find **Chapter Heading Style**\n3. Choose your preferred option\n\nFor Verse Novels, book headings follow the same options (e.g., \"Book I\", \"Book I: Origins\").\n\n## Scene Breaks\n\nWhen scenes transition within a chapter, control the separator:\n\n| Break Style | Appearance |\n|-------------|------------|\n| **Page Break** | Each scene starts on a new page |\n| **Section Mark** | Centred symbol (e.g., * * * or §) between scenes |\n| **Double Space** | Extra blank line between scenes |\n| **None** | Scenes flow together with no visible break |\n\nFor most fiction, **Section Mark** (e.g., * * *) is the standard scene break convention. **Page Break** is more common between chapters than between scenes.\n\n## First Paragraph Style\n\nThe first paragraph of a chapter or scene can have special formatting:\n\n| Style | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **No indent** | Traditional — first paragraph is flush left |\n| **Drop cap** | Decorative large initial letter |\n| **Standard indent** | Same indent as all other paragraphs |\n\nMost publishers expect **no indent** for the first paragraph after a chapter heading or scene break.\n\n## Footnote Numbering\n\nChoose how footnotes are numbered across the manuscript:\n\n- **Per File**: Numbering restarts in each scene\n- **Continuous**: Sequential numbering across the entire manuscript\n\n## Industry Standard Manuscript Format\n\nFor submissions to agents and publishers, standard manuscript format is expected:\n\n### Typical Requirements\n- Double-spaced text\n- 12pt Courier or Times New Roman\n- 1-inch (2.54 cm) margins\n- Header with author name, title, and page number\n- Word count on the first page\n- Scene breaks indicated with # or * * *\n\n### Applying Standard Format\n1. Configure your **Stylesheet** for manuscript style (double-spaced, standard font)\n2. Set **Page Setup** for Letter or A4 paper with 1-inch margins\n3. Set scene break style to **Section Mark**\n4. Export to PDF or RTF\n\n## Formatting for Different Purposes\n\n### Agent/Publisher Submission\n- Standard manuscript format (above)\n- Clean, professional appearance\n- Follow specific submission guidelines if provided\n\n### Beta Readers\n- Easy-to-read formatting\n- Include page numbers\n- PDF for consistency across devices\n\n### Self-Publishing\n- A starting point — further formatting will be needed\n- Platform-specific requirements vary (Kindle, IngramSpark, etc.)\n\n### Personal Review\n- Your preference\n- Consider larger text for proofing\n- Print a paper copy for marking up\n\n## Export Formats\n\nFiction manuscripts can be exported in all available formats:\n\n| Format | Best For |\n|--------|----------|\n| **PDF** | Submissions, sharing, print — looks identical everywhere |\n| **RTF** | Editor collaboration — editable in Word |\n| **HTML** | Web publishing, online sharing |\n| **Markdown** | Plain text archives, version control |\n\nSee [Export Options](../9-publishing/91-export-options.md) for full details.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### Scenes Out of Order\n- Check scene order within the chapter\n- Verify chapter order in the Chapters list\n- Review in the manuscript preview\n\n### Missing Scenes\n- Ensure scenes are assigned to chapters (Novel and Verse Novel)\n- Ensure scenes have the correct workflow status\n- Check that **Include in Manuscript** is toggled on\n\n### Chapter Breaks Wrong\n- Check chapter heading settings\n- Ensure chapters are defined and ordered correctly\n- Review in pagination view\n\n### Formatting Issues\n- Review your stylesheet settings\n- Check paragraph styles are applied correctly\n- Verify page setup settings\n\n## See Also\n- [Fiction Mode Overview](71-fiction-mode-overview.md)\n- [Organizing Your Fiction](72-organizing-your-fiction.md)\n- [Scene Organization](73-scene-organization.md)\n- [Manuscript Structure](../3-projects/36-manuscript-structure.md)\n- [Export Options](../9-publishing/91-export-options.md)\n- [PDF Export](../9-publishing/92-pdf-export.md)\n\n---\n",
              "createdDate": "2026-02-03T15:03:56Z",
              "footnotes": [],
              "id": "856E2BD4-2481-4082-9FD1-576CE977A38A",
              "versionNumber": 1
            }
