          "modifiedDate": "2026-02-03T15:04:31Z",
          "name": "73-scene-organization",
          "sectionId": "9024754E-EEB6-4B61-8390-BF5FD3CA8E54",
          "userOrder": 2,
          "versions": [
            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Scene Organization\n\nScenes are the fundamental building blocks of fiction in Writing Shed Pro. This guide covers creating, organizing, and linking scenes effectively.\n\n## Understanding Scenes\n\nA scene is a continuous unit of action that:\n- Happens in one place and time\n- Has a purpose (advance plot, reveal character, etc.)\n- Ends with a change or outcome\n\n## Creating Scenes\n\n### In the Draft Folder\n1. Open the **Draft** folder\n2. Tap **+**\n3. Enter a scene title\n4. Tap **Create**\n\nThe scene opens in the editor.\n\n### Scene Titles\nUse titles that help you identify scenes:\n- \"Opening - Jake's apartment\" (descriptive)\n- \"Jake meets Sarah\" (action-based)\n- \"1.1\" (numerical, if you prefer)\n\nYou can always rename later.\n\n## Scene Status\n\nScenes move through workflow folders:\n\n| Folder | Status | Meaning |\n|--------|--------|---------|\n| Draft | Work in progress | Still being written/revised |\n| Ready | Complete | Polished and final |\n| Set Aside | On hold | Not currently working on |\n| Trash | Deleted | Can be restored |\n\n### Moving Scenes\n1. Long-press the scene\n2. Select **Move to...**\n3. Choose the destination folder\n\n## Linking Scenes to Plot Elements\n\nConnect scenes to your story structure:\n\n### From the Scene\n1. Open the scene\n2. Access scene details\n3. Tap **Link to Plot Element**\n4. Select the relevant plot element(s)\n\n### From the Plot Element\n1. Open a plot element\n2. Tap **Create Scene**\n3. A new scene is created and linked\n\n### Why Link?\n- Track story structure coverage\n- Ensure all plot points have scenes\n- Navigate between related content\n- Visualize your story's shape\n\n## Scene Details\n\nEach scene can store metadata:\n\n### Basic Info\n- Title\n- Status (Draft/Ready/etc.)\n- Word count\n- Creation date\n\n### Story Connections\n- Linked plot elements\n- Associated characters\n- Location\n\n### Notes\n- Scene summary\n- Purpose/goal\n- Revision notes\n\n## Viewing Scenes\n\n### In Folder Lists\n- See title and preview\n- Word count displayed\n- Status indicated by folder\n\n### Scene Detail View\n- Full scene content\n- All metadata\n- Linked elements\n\n## Reordering Scenes\n\n### Within a Folder\n1. Tap **Edit**\n2. Drag scenes to new positions\n3. Tap **Done**\n\n### In Chapters\nScene order within chapters determines manuscript order. See [Chapter Management](02-chapter-management.md).\n\n## Finding Scenes\n\n### Browse by Folder\n- Draft, Ready, Set Aside contain all scenes\n\n### Search\n- Use project search to find scenes by title or content\n\n### By Plot Element\n- Open a plot element to see linked scenes\n\n### By Character/Location\n- Open a character or location to see associated scenes\n\n## Scene Best Practices\n\n### Write First, Organize Later\nDon't over-organize while drafting. Write scenes, then sort them into chapters.\n\n### Use Status Meaningfully\n- Draft = still working\n- Ready = done (for now)\n- Set Aside = maybe later\n\n### Keep Scenes Focused\nEach scene should have one primary purpose. If a scene does too much, consider splitting.\n\n### Track Progress\nMove scenes through the workflow as you complete them. It's motivating to see Ready fill up.\n\n## Scenes and Plot\n\n### Plotters\n- Create plot elements first\n- Generate scenes from plot elements\n- Write to fill the structure\n\n### Pantsers\n- Write scenes as inspiration strikes\n- Create plot elements retrospectively\n- Link to discover your structure\n\n### Plantsers\n- Create loose plot elements\n- Write scenes freely\n- Adjust structure as you go\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### Can't Find a Scene\n- Check all folders (Draft, Ready, Set Aside, Trash)\n- Use search\n- Check chapter contents\n\n### Scene in Wrong Folder\n- Long-press and select Move to...\n- Or drag and drop\n\n### Lost Scene Content\n- Check version history\n- Content auto-saves frequently\n- Look in Trash if deleted\n\n## See Also\n- [Fiction Mode Overview](01-fiction-mode-overview.md)\n- [Chapter Management](02-chapter-management.md)\n- [Manuscript Formatting](04-manuscript-formatting.md)\n\n---\n",
              "createdDate": "2026-02-03T15:03:20Z",
              "footnotes": [],
              "id": "A67F507D-2DE8-4679-BAED-4BAF9ED73063",
              "versionNumber": 1
            }
