              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Forms and Templates\n\nWriting Shed Pro includes templates for over 30 traditional poetry forms. This guide lists available forms and explains how to use templates effectively.\n\n## Using Form Templates\n\n### Creating a Poem with a Template\n1. In a Poetry project, open Draft folder\n2. Tap **+** to create a new poem\n3. Enter a title\n4. Select a form from the dropdown\n5. Tap **Create**\n\nThe poem opens with the form's structure as a guide.\n\n### What Templates Provide\n- Line placeholders\n- Rhyme scheme indication\n- Syllable count targets\n- Form reference information\n\n### Templates as Guides\nTemplates show structure—they don't enforce it. You can:\n- Follow the template exactly\n- Adapt it to your needs\n- Delete placeholders and write freely\n\n## Japanese Forms\n\n### Haiku\n- **Lines**: 3\n- **Syllables**: 5-7-5 (17 total)\n- **Subject**: Nature, seasons, single moment\n- **Special**: No rhyme, no title traditionally\n\n### Tanka\n- **Lines**: 5\n- **Syllables**: 5-7-5-7-7 (31 total)\n- **Structure**: Haiku-like opening + emotional reflection\n- **Special**: Pivot line creates shift\n\n### Senryū\n- **Lines**: 3\n- **Syllables**: 5-7-5\n- **Subject**: Human nature, humor, irony\n- **Special**: Haiku structure, different focus\n\n### Renga/Renku\n- **Lines**: Variable (linked verses)\n- **Structure**: Alternating 5-7-5 and 7-7 sections\n- **Special**: Collaborative form\n\n## Sonnets\n\n### Shakespearean (English)\n- **Lines**: 14\n- **Meter**: Iambic pentameter\n- **Rhyme**: ABAB CDCD EFEF GG\n- **Structure**: 3 quatrains + couplet\n\n### Petrarchan (Italian)\n- **Lines**: 14\n- **Meter**: Iambic pentameter\n- **Rhyme**: ABBAABBA CDECDE (or CDCDCD)\n- **Structure**: Octave + sestet with volta\n\n### Spenserian\n- **Lines**: 14\n- **Meter**: Iambic pentameter\n- **Rhyme**: ABAB BCBC CDCD EE\n- **Structure**: Interlocking rhymes + couplet\n\n## French Forms\n\n### Villanelle\n- **Lines**: 19\n- **Structure**: 5 tercets + 1 quatrain\n- **Rhyme**: ABA ABA ABA ABA ABA ABAA\n- **Special**: Lines 1 and 3 repeat as refrains\n\n### Pantoum\n- **Lines**: Variable (multiples of 4)\n- **Structure**: Interlocking quatrains\n- **Rhyme**: ABAB (lines 2,4 become 1,3 of next stanza)\n- **Special**: Last stanza uses first lines\n\n### Rondeau\n- **Lines**: 15\n- **Structure**: 3 stanzas (5-4-6 lines)\n- **Rhyme**: AABBA AABR AABBAR\n- **Special**: Rentrement (refrain from opening)\n\n### Ballade\n- **Lines**: 28 (3 stanzas of 8 + 4-line envoi)\n- **Rhyme**: ABABBCBC (envoi: BCBC)\n- **Special**: Refrain ends each stanza\n\n### Triolet\n- **Lines**: 8\n- **Rhyme**: ABaAabAB\n- **Special**: Lines 1, 4, 7 identical; Lines 2, 8 identical\n\n## Welsh Forms\n\n### Cywydd\n- **Lines**: Couplets\n- **Syllables**: 7 per line\n- **Rhyme**: One stressed, one unstressed per couplet\n- **Special**: Complex sound patterns (cynghanedd)\n\n### Englyn\n- **Lines**: 4\n- **Syllables**: 30 total (10-6-7-7 traditional)\n- **Special**: Pivot in first line\n\n## English Forms\n\n### Limerick\n- **Lines**: 5\n- **Rhyme**: AABBA\n- **Meter**: Anapestic\n- **Special**: Humorous content\n\n### Ballad\n- **Lines**: Quatrains\n- **Rhyme**: ABAB or ABCB\n- **Meter**: Alternating tetrameter/trimeter\n- **Special**: Narrative, often with refrain\n\n### Blank Verse\n- **Lines**: Variable\n- **Meter**: Iambic pentameter\n- **Rhyme**: None\n- **Special**: Drama, epic, meditative poetry\n\n### Heroic Couplets\n- **Lines**: Couplets\n- **Meter**: Iambic pentameter\n- **Rhyme**: AA BB CC...\n- **Special**: Augustan poetry, wit\n\n## Modern Forms\n\n### Free Verse\n- No fixed structure\n- No required rhyme or meter\n- Emphasis on rhythm, image, meaning\n- Most common contemporary form\n\n### Prose Poetry\n- Block paragraphs\n- No line breaks\n- Poetic language and imagery\n- Blurs poetry/prose boundary\n\n### Concrete/Visual Poetry\n- Shape matters\n- Words form images\n- Spatial arrangement significant\n\n## Complex Forms\n\n### Sestina\n- **Lines**: 39 (6 six-line stanzas + 3-line envoi)\n- **Rhyme**: None (word repetition)\n- **Special**: 6 end words rotate in set pattern\n\n### Crown of Sonnets\n- **Lines**: 98 (7 linked sonnets)\n- **Structure**: Each sonnet's last line becomes next's first\n- **Special**: Final sonnet uses all first lines\n\n### Ghazal\n- **Lines**: Couplets (5-15)\n- **Rhyme**: AA BA CA DA...\n- **Special**: Refrain + signature in final couplet\n\n## Working with Templates\n\n### Start Simple\nBegin with shorter forms (haiku, limerick) before tackling villanelles or sestinas.\n\n### Read Examples\nStudy published poems in your chosen form. Understanding comes from exposure.\n\n### Break the Rules Knowingly\nLearn the form first, then decide when to deviate. Informed rule-breaking is creative; ignorant rule-breaking is sloppy.\n\n### Use Templates Repeatedly\nWrite many poems in a form to truly learn it. One sonnet is practice; twenty is mastery.\n\n## See Also\n- [Poetry Mode Overview](01-poetry-mode-overview.md)\n- [Syllable Counting](02-syllable-counting.md)\n- [Rhyme Tools](03-rhyme-tools.md)\n- [Verse Highlighting](04-verse-highlighting.md)\n\n---\n",
              "createdDate": "2026-02-03T14:57:32Z",
