          "includedInManuscript": true,
          "modifiedDate": "2026-02-03T14:42:13Z",
          "name": "31-project-types-overview",
          "sectionId": "C22A721E-30BE-427A-906A-A6167E4071E9",
          "userOrder": 0,
          "versions": [
            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Project Types Overview\n\nWriting Shed Pro offers four project types, each designed for a specific kind of writing. Choosing the right type gives you access to specialized tools that make your writing process more efficient.\n\n## The Four Project Types\n\n| Project Type | Best For | Key Features |\n|--------------|----------|--------------|\n| **Prose** | Essays, articles, general writing | Simple organization, flexible structure |\n| **Poetry** | Poems and verse | Syllable counting, rhyme tools, form templates |\n| **Fiction** | Novels and short stories | Scenes, chapters, plot elements, characters |\n| **Drama** | Scripts and plays | Script formatting, DML markup, film/stage output |\n\n## Prose Projects\n\nProse projects are the most flexible type, suitable for any writing that doesn't fit the specialized categories.\n\n### Use For\n- Essays and academic writing\n- Blog posts and articles\n- Personal journals\n- Technical documentation\n- Any general-purpose writing\n\n### Folder Structure\n- Draft\n- Ready\n- Set Aside\n- Trash\n\n### Features\n- Standard text formatting\n- Footnotes and comments\n- Images with captions\n- Flexible folder organization\n\n## Poetry Projects\n\nPoetry projects include specialized tools for writing verse in both free and formal styles.\n\n### Use For\n- Traditional forms (sonnets, haiku, villanelles, etc.)\n- Free verse\n- Song lyrics\n- Any metered or structured verse\n\n### Folder Structure\n- Poems (your writing area)\n- Manuscript (Front Matter, Body Matter, Back Matter)\n- Collections (Poetry Collections — first-class grouping of poems)\n- Submissions\n- Research\n- Magazines\n- Competitions\n- Other\n- Trash\n\n### Features\n- **Form Templates**: Start with the structure of 30+ poetry forms\n- **Syllable Counting**: Real-time syllable counts per line\n- **Stress Analysis**: View stress patterns for metered verse\n- **Rhyme Detection**: See rhyme schemes as you write\n- **Form Reference**: Quick reference for the rules of each form\n- **Collections**: Group related poems together\n- **Submissions**: Track where you've sent your work\n\nSee [Poetry Mode Overview](../6-poetry-features/61-poetry-mode-overview.md) for details.\n\n## Fiction Projects\n\nFiction projects help you organize and write novels and short stories with support for scenes, chapters, and plot elements.\n\n### Fiction Classes\nWhen creating a fiction project, you choose a class:\n\n| Class | Structure | Use For |\n|-------|-----------|---------|\n| **Novel** | Chapters contain Scenes | Long-form fiction |\n| **Verse Novel** | Books contain Episodes (verse) | Novel-length poetry |\n| **Short Fiction** | Scenes only (no chapters) | Short stories, novellas |\n\n### Folder Structure\n- Manuscript (Front Matter, Body Matter, Back Matter)\n- Scenes (your writing, filtered by workflow status)\n- Chapters (Novel), Books (Verse Novel), or flat (Short Fiction)\n- Plot Elements\n- Characters\n- Locations\n- Submissions\n- Research\n- Publishers / Agents\n- Other\n- Trash\n\n### Story Structures\nChoose a story structure to help organize your plot:\n- **Freeform**: No predefined structure\n- **Three-Act**: Classic beginning, middle, end\n- **Monomyth (Vogler)**: 12-stage Hero's Journey\n- **Monomyth (Campbell)**: 17-stage mythic structure\n\n### Features\n- **Scene Management**: Create and organize scenes with status tracking\n- **Plot Outlining**: Map story beats to structure stages\n- **Character Profiles**: Track character details and arcs\n- **Location Database**: Define settings and places\n- **Scene-Plot Linking**: Connect scenes to plot elements\n- **Manuscript Assembly**: Combine chapters into a complete manuscript\n\nSee [Fiction Mode Overview](../7-fiction-features/71-fiction-mode-overview.md) for details.\n\n## Drama Projects\n\nDrama projects provide script formatting tools for playwrights and screenwriters.\n\n### Use For\n- Stage plays\n- Screenplays\n- Teleplays\n- Radio plays\n\n### Folder Structure\n- Manuscript (Front Matter, Body Matter, Back Matter)\n- Scenes (your writing, filtered by workflow status)\n- Acts (group scenes into acts)\n- Plot Elements\n- Characters\n- Locations\n- Submissions\n- Research\n- Publishers / Agents\n- Other\n- Trash\n\n### Output Formats\nChoose how your script is formatted:\n- **Film/Screenplay**: Industry-standard screenplay format\n- **Stage Play**: Traditional stage script format\n\n### Features\n- **Drama Markup Language (DML)**: Easy markup for dialogue and directions\n- **Auto-Formatting**: Character names, dialogue, parentheticals formatted automatically\n- **Dual Output**: Same source renders as film or stage format\n- **Scene Headings**: INT./EXT. or Act/Scene formats\n- **Transitions**: CUT TO, FADE OUT, BLACKOUT\n\nSee [Drama Mode Overview](../8-drama-features/81-drama-mode-overview.md) for details.\n\n## Choosing a Project Type\n\n### When You're Unsure\n- Start with **Prose** if you're not writing poetry, fiction, or scripts\n- You can always create a new project of the right type later\n- Projects can't change type after creation\n\n### Multiple Types of Writing\nCreate separate projects for different kinds of work:\n- One Poetry project for all your poems\n- One Fiction project per novel\n- One Drama project per screenplay\n\n### Can I Change a Project's Type?\nNo. The project type is set at creation and cannot be changed. This is because each type has a different data structure optimized for that kind of writing.\n\nIf you realize you need a different type:\n1. Create a new project of the correct type\n2. Copy your text content to the new project\n3. Delete the old project if no longer needed\n\n## See Also\n- [Creating Projects](32-creating-projects.md)\n- [Poetry Mode Overview](../6-poetry-features/61-poetry-mode-overview.md)\n- [Fiction Mode Overview](../7-fiction-features/71-fiction-mode-overview.md)\n- [Drama Mode Overview](../8-drama-features/81-drama-mode-overview.md)",
              "createdDate": "2026-02-03T14:38:10Z",
              "footnotes": [],
              "id": "53DD423F-8DCA-42E9-9911-D7A479FA0F64",
              "versionNumber": 1
            }
          ],
          "workflowStatus": "ready"
        },
        {
          "contentTypeRaw": "markdown",
          "createdDate": "2026-02-03T14:54:23Z",
          "currentVersionIndex": 0,
          "id": "262E6916-F033-4189-B4ED-7D45CA87A0B2",
          "includedInManuscript": true,
          "modifiedDate": "2026-02-11T12:00:00Z",
          "name": "61-poetry-mode-overview",
          "sectionId": "1D9D3C55-2A6A-4AF7-AFC3-F81876599A63",
          "userOrder": 0,
          "versions": [
            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Poetry Mode Overview\n\nPoetry projects in Writing Shed Pro provide specialized tools for poets working in both traditional forms and free verse. This overview explains what's available and how to use these features.\n\n## What Makes Poetry Mode Special\n\nPoetry mode understands that poets have unique needs:\n- **Structure matters**: Syllable counts, line breaks, stanza organization\n- **Sound matters**: Rhyme schemes, stress patterns, meter\n- **Form matters**: Templates for sonnets, haiku, villanelles, and more\n- **Precision matters**: Every word counts\n\n## Creating a Poetry Project\n\n1. From the project list, tap **+**\n2. Enter a project name\n3. Select **Poetry** as the project type\n4. Tap **Create**\n\n## Poetry Project Structure\n\nPoetry projects include specialized folders:\n\n| Folder | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Poems** | Your main writing area — all poems live here |\n| **Manuscript** | Front Matter, Body Matter, and Back Matter for export |\n| **Collections** | Poetry Collections — group related poems together |\n| **Submissions** | Track where you've sent work |\n| **Research** | Reference material |\n| **Magazines** | Publication targets |\n| **Competitions** | Contest targets |\n| **Other** | Miscellaneous files |\n| **Trash** | Deleted poems (restorable) |\n\n## Creating a Poem\n\n### With a Form Template\n1. Open the Draft folder\n2. Tap **+**\n3. Enter a title\n4. Select a poetry form from the dropdown\n5. Tap **Create**\n\nThe poem opens with the form's structure as a guide.\n\n### Free Verse\n1. Open the Draft folder\n2. Tap **+**\n3. Enter a title\n4. Select **Free Verse** or leave form blank\n5. Tap **Create**\n\nStart with a blank page.\n\n## Available Poetry Forms\n\nWriting Shed Pro includes templates for 30+ traditional forms:\n\n### Short Forms\n- **Haiku** (5-7-5 syllables)\n- **Tanka** (5-7-5-7-7 syllables)\n- **Senryū** (5-7-5 syllables, human nature focus)\n\n### Sonnets\n- **Shakespearean** (ABAB CDCD EFEF GG)\n- **Petrarchan** (ABBAABBA CDECDE)\n- **Spenserian** (ABAB BCBC CDCD EE)\n\n### Complex Forms\n- **Villanelle** (19 lines, repeated lines)\n- **Pantoum** (interlocking quatrains)\n- **Sestina** (39 lines, word repetition)\n- **Ghazal** (couplets with refrain)\n\n### Other Forms\n- **Limerick** (5 lines, AABBA)\n- **Ballad** (quatrains, ABAB or ABCB)\n- **Ode** (varied structure)\n- **Free Verse** (no constraints)\n\nSee [Forms and Templates](65-forms-and-templates.md) for the complete list.\n\n## Poetry Tools in the Editor\n\nWhen editing a poem, additional tools are available:\n\n### Syllable Counter\nEach line shows an estimated syllable count:\n- Appears at the line's right edge\n- Updates as you type\n- Helps maintain syllabic forms\n\n### Stress Pattern Display\nFor metered verse, view the stress pattern:\n- Unstressed syllables: ˘\n- Stressed syllables: ´\n- Helps identify rhythm issues\n\n### Form Reference Panel\nAccess the rules for your selected form:\n- Tap the **Form Info** button\n- See line counts, rhyme schemes, other requirements\n- Keep visible while writing\n\n## Working with Rhyme\n\n### Rhyme Scheme Display\nSome forms show the expected rhyme scheme:\n- Letters indicate rhyming lines (A, B, C, etc.)\n- Helps you track the pattern\n\n### Rhyme Suggestions (if available)\nWhen seeking rhymes:\n- Highlight a word\n- Access rhyme tools\n- See suggested rhyming words\n\nSee [Rhyme Tools](63-rhyme-tools.md) for details.\n\n## Poetry Workflow\n\n### Drafting\n1. Choose a form (or free verse)\n2. Use the template as a guide\n3. Write without worrying about perfection\n4. Check metrics as you go\n\n### Revising\n1. Review syllable counts\n2. Check stress patterns\n3. Verify rhyme scheme\n4. Read aloud (essential!)\n\n### Polishing\n1. Move to Ready when satisfied\n2. Create Poetry Collections for related poems\n3. Add collections to manuscript body matter for export\n4. Submit to publications\n\n## Poetry Collections\n\nPoetry Collections are first-class entities in Poetry projects. Each collection has its own name, synopsis, and list of linked poems.\n\n### Creating a Collection\n1. Open the **Collections** folder\n2. Tap **+**\n3. Enter a name and optional synopsis\n4. Tap **Create**\n\n### Adding Poems\n1. Navigate to poems in the Poems folder\n2. Select one or more poems\n3. Choose **Add to Collection...**\n4. Select the target collection\n\n### Body Matter Integration\nCollections can be included in your manuscript's body matter:\n1. Open the **Manuscript** folder → **Body Matter**\n2. Add collections (and individual poems) to body matter\n3. Reorder as needed\n4. Collections are assembled in order during PDF/manuscript export\n\nSee [Poetry Collections](../10-advanced-features/104-collections.md) for more details.\n\n## Tips for Poetry Mode\n\n### Use Forms as Guides\nTemplates help but don't constrain. Break the rules when the poem demands it.\n\n### Trust the Metrics\nSyllable counts are estimates—English pronunciation varies. Your ear is the final judge.\n\n### Read Aloud\nNo tool replaces hearing your poem. Read it aloud during revision.\n\n### Organize by Theme\nUse subfolders or collections to group poems:\n- By season or period\n- By subject\n- By form\n- By submission target\n\n## See Also\n- [Syllable Counting](62-syllable-counting.md)\n- [Rhyme Tools](63-rhyme-tools.md)\n- [Verse Highlighting](64-verse-highlighting.md)\n- [Forms and Templates](65-forms-and-templates.md)\n- [Manuscript and Export](66-manuscript-and-export.md)",
