        {
          "contentTypeRaw": "markdown",
          "createdDate": "2026-02-03T14:55:11Z",
          "currentVersionIndex": 0,
          "id": "E41C160C-DCC8-46E0-8FD5-548FDB51EBA1",
          "includedInManuscript": true,
          "modifiedDate": "2026-02-03T15:01:16Z",
          "name": "62-syllable-counting",
          "sectionId": "1D9D3C55-2A6A-4AF7-AFC3-F81876599A63",
          "userOrder": 1,
          "versions": [
            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Syllable Counting\n\nSyllable counting is essential for many poetry forms. Writing Shed Pro provides automatic syllable counting to help you maintain proper meter and form requirements.\n\n## Understanding Syllable Counts\n\nMany poetry forms require specific syllable counts:\n- **Haiku**: 5-7-5 syllables\n- **Tanka**: 5-7-5-7-7 syllables\n- **Limerick**: Variable, roughly 8-8-5-5-8\n\nWriting Shed Pro counts syllables automatically, displaying the count for each line.\n\n## Viewing Syllable Counts\n\nWhen editing a poem with a syllable-based form:\n\n1. Syllable counts appear at the right edge of each line\n2. Counts update as you type\n3. Compare against your form's requirements\n\n### Visual Indicators\n- Numbers show the syllable count\n- Colors may indicate match/mismatch with form requirements\n- Form reference panel shows expected counts\n\n## How Syllable Counting Works\n\nWriting Shed Pro uses a sophisticated syllable counting algorithm:\n\n### Pronunciation Dictionaries\n- **CMU Dictionary**: Standard American English pronunciations\n- **British Dictionary**: UK English pronunciations with different stress patterns\n- Provides accurate syllable counts for known words\n\n### Selecting Your Dialect\nWriting Shed Pro supports both American and British English pronunciation:\n\n1. Go to the main menu (gear icon)\n2. Open **Poetry Settings**\n3. Select **Pronunciation** → Choose **US** or **UK**\n\nThis affects:\n- Syllable counting (some words have different syllable counts)\n- Stress pattern analysis\n- Rhyme detection\n\n#### Key Differences\n| Word | American | British |\n|------|----------|---------|\n| secretary | 4 syllables | 3 syllables |\n| laboratory | 5 syllables | 4 syllables |\n| advertisement | ad-ver-TISE-ment | ad-VER-tis-ment |\n| controversy | CON-tro-ver-sy | con-TROV-er-sy |\n\n### Fallback Algorithm\nFor words not in the dictionary:\n- Estimates based on vowel patterns\n- Handles most cases well\n- May vary from your intended pronunciation\n\n## Accuracy Considerations\n\nSyllable counting is an estimate, not absolute truth:\n\n### Variables in English\n- Regional pronunciation differs\n- Poetic license allows variation\n- Some words have multiple valid pronunciations\n\n### Examples of Variation\n- \"Fire\": 1 or 2 syllables?\n- \"Every\": 2 or 3 syllables?\n- \"Comfortable\": 3 or 4 syllables?\n\n### Your Ear is Final\nThe count is a guide. If you hear 3 syllables, use 3—regardless of what the tool says.\n\n## Syllable Counting Tips\n\n### Trust but Verify\nUse the count as a starting point, then read aloud to confirm.\n\n### Watch for Problem Words\nLearn which words the algorithm misses in your vocabulary.\n\n### Consider Elision\nIn poetry, syllables can be elided (combined):\n- \"O'er\" for \"over\"\n- \"E'en\" for \"even\"\n- Write as you intend to read\n\n### Compound Words\nThe algorithm handles most compounds, but check unusual ones.\n\n## Working with Syllable Counts\n\n### Adjusting Line Length\nIf a line is too long:\n- Find a shorter synonym\n- Restructure the phrase\n- Consider elision\n\nIf a line is too short:\n- Add modifiers\n- Choose longer synonyms\n- Expand the imagery\n\n### Maintaining Form\nFor strict forms like haiku:\n1. Write your thought\n2. Check the count\n3. Adjust word choices\n4. Verify by reading aloud\n\n### Breaking Form\nSometimes a line needs an extra syllable for the right word. Many poets prioritize meaning over strict count. Know the rules, then decide when to break them.\n\n## Syllables and Meter\n\nSyllable count differs from meter:\n- **Syllables**: How many beats\n- **Meter**: Which beats are stressed\n\nFor metered verse, see [Verse Highlighting](04-verse-highlighting.md) for stress pattern analysis.\n\n### Example\n\"To be or not to be\" has 6 syllables with alternating stress (iambic trimeter):\n```\nto BE or NOT to BE\n˘  ´  ˘  ´   ˘  ´\n```\n\n## Disabling Syllable Counts\n\nIf you find the counts distracting:\n1. Toggle off syllable display in poem settings\n2. Re-enable when needed for checking\n\nThe feature is there to help—use it when helpful, hide it when not.\n\n## See Also\n- [Poetry Mode Overview](01-poetry-mode-overview.md)\n- [Verse Highlighting](04-verse-highlighting.md)\n- [Forms and Templates](05-forms-and-templates.md)\n\n---\n",
              "createdDate": "2026-02-03T14:55:11Z",
              "footnotes": [],
              "id": "2BB849EB-30D1-45A3-92F3-D0A7F9ED6CD4",
              "versionNumber": 1
            }
