          "userOrder": 3,
          "versions": [
            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Verse Highlighting\n\nFor poets working with metered verse, Writing Shed Pro provides stress pattern analysis to help you maintain consistent rhythm.\n\n## Understanding Meter\n\nMeter is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in a line of poetry:\n\n- **Unstressed** (˘): Lighter, shorter sounds\n- **Stressed** (´): Heavier, emphasized sounds\n\n### Common Meters\n\n| Meter | Pattern | Example |\n|-------|---------|---------|\n| **Iambic** | ˘ ´ | \"a-LONE\" |\n| **Trochaic** | ´ ˘ | \"GAR-den\" |\n| **Anapestic** | ˘ ˘ ´ | \"in-ter-VENE\" |\n| **Dactylic** | ´ ˘ ˘ | \"MER-ri-ly\" |\n| **Spondaic** | ´ ´ | \"HEART-BREAK\" |\n\n### Feet Per Line\n\nThe number of feet determines the line length:\n- Trimeter: 3 feet\n- Tetrameter: 4 feet\n- Pentameter: 5 feet\n- Hexameter: 6 feet\n\n**Example**: Iambic pentameter = 5 iambic feet (˘ ´ ˘ ´ ˘ ´ ˘ ´ ˘ ´)\n\n## Viewing Stress Patterns\n\nWhen stress analysis is enabled:\n\n1. Each line shows its detected stress pattern\n2. Symbols appear above or below syllables\n3. Compare against expected meter\n\n### Visual Display\n```\nto BE or NOT to BE that IS the QUES tion\n˘  ´  ˘  ´   ˘  ´   ˘   ´  ˘   ´    ˘\n```\n\n### Enabling Stress Analysis\n1. Open poem settings or form reference\n2. Enable \"Show Stress Pattern\"\n3. Patterns appear for each line\n\n## How Stress Detection Works\n\nWriting Shed Pro uses:\n\n### Dictionary-Based Analysis\n- Pronunciation dictionaries provide stress markers for known words\n- **American English**: CMU Pronouncing Dictionary\n- **British English**: Specialized UK pronunciation dictionary\n- High accuracy for standard pronunciation in your selected dialect\n\n### Dialect Selection\nStress patterns can differ significantly between American and British English. See [Syllable Counting](02-syllable-counting.md#selecting-your-dialect) for how to switch between dialects.\n\n#### Stress Differences by Dialect\n| Word | American | British |\n|------|----------|---------|\n| advertisement | ad-ver-TISE-ment | ad-VER-tis-ment |\n| controversy | CON-tro-ver-sy | con-TROV-er-sy |\n| primarily | pry-MARE-uh-lee | PRY-muh-ruh-lee |\n| research | REE-search / ree-SEARCH | REE-search |\n\nThe stress pattern display automatically reflects your chosen dialect.\n\n### Contextual Estimation\n- Some words change stress based on usage\n- \"record\" (noun: RE-cord) vs. \"record\" (verb: re-CORD)\n- Algorithm uses common patterns\n\n### Limitations\n- Estimates, not certainties\n- Pronunciation varies by dialect\n- Poetic emphasis may differ from speech\n\n## Working with Stress Patterns\n\n### Verifying Meter\nFor formal verse:\n1. Write your line\n2. Check the stress display\n3. Read aloud to confirm\n4. Adjust problem areas\n\n### Common Issues\n\n**Wrong stress on a word**\n- Try a synonym\n- Rearrange the phrase\n- Consider if the \"error\" works poetically\n\n**Extra or missing syllables**\n- Adjust word choices\n- Use elision when appropriate\n- Consider metrical substitution\n\n### Metrical Substitution\nPoets regularly substitute feet for effect:\n- A trochee in iambic verse for emphasis\n- A spondee for weight\n- A pyrrhic (˘ ˘) for lightness\n\nGood poetry varies the beat—perfect regularity can sound mechanical.\n\n## Stress in Different Forms\n\n### Sonnets\nTraditionally iambic pentameter:\n- 10 syllables per line\n- Alternating unstressed-stressed\n- Substitutions common and encouraged\n\n### Blank Verse\nIambic pentameter without rhyme:\n- Shakespeare's plays\n- Milton's Paradise Lost\n- Flexibility within the pattern\n\n### Ballad Meter\nAlternating tetrameter and trimeter:\n- Common in folk songs\n- \"Common meter\" in hymns\n\n### Free Verse\nNo required meter:\n- Use stress for emphasis\n- Create rhythmic phrases\n- Contrast regular and irregular lines\n\n## Tips for Metered Verse\n\n### Read Shakespeare\nThe master of iambic pentameter. Note where he follows and breaks the pattern.\n\n### Read Aloud\nAlways. The stress display is a guide—your voice is the test.\n\n### Embrace Variation\nPerfect meter sounds like a metronome. Variation creates life.\n\n### Trust Your Ear\nIf it sounds right, it probably is—regardless of what the analysis shows.\n\n### Learn Natural Stress\nEnglish has natural rhythms. Learn which words are typically stressed.\n\n## Disabling Stress Display\n\nIf the stress marks distract you:\n1. Toggle off in poem settings\n2. Re-enable when checking meter\n3. Use as a tool, not a constant presence\n\n## See Also\n- [Poetry Mode Overview](01-poetry-mode-overview.md)\n- [Syllable Counting](02-syllable-counting.md)\n- [Forms and Templates](05-forms-and-templates.md)\n\n---\n",
