          "versions": [
            {
              "comments": [],
              "content": "# Manuscript and Export (Poetry)\n\nThis guide covers how Writing Shed Pro assembles Poetry project content into a manuscript for export.\n\n## How Poetry Manuscripts Are Assembled\n\nPoetry manuscripts use a **flat** assembly model:\n\n1. **Front Matter** is placed first (Title Page, Copyright, etc.)\n2. **All poems** are assembled in display order\n3. **Back Matter** is appended (Endnotes, Glossary, Bibliography, Index)\n\n### Poem Ordering\n\nPoems appear in the manuscript in their **display order** — the order you see them in the file list. To change the order:\n1. Tap **Edit** in the file list\n2. Drag poems to reorder using the grip handles\n3. Tap **Done**\n\nThe manuscript follows this order exactly.\n\n### What Gets Included\n\nBy default, all poems are included. You can exclude individual poems:\n1. Open the poem's context menu\n2. Toggle **Include in Manuscript** off\n3. The poem stays in your project but is skipped during assembly\n\nThis is useful for unfinished work, experiments, or poems earmarked for a different collection.\n\n## Previewing the Manuscript\n\nBefore exporting:\n1. Open the **Manuscript** view\n2. Review the assembled content\n3. Check that poems appear in the correct order\n4. Verify formatting is preserved\n5. Confirm no unwanted poems are included\n\n## Poetry Formatting in the Manuscript\n\nPoetry formatting is preserved during manuscript assembly:\n- **Line breaks** are maintained exactly as written\n- **Stanza breaks** (blank lines between stanzas) are preserved\n- **Indentation** is carried through to the output\n- **Character formatting** (bold, italic) is retained\n\n### Poem Breaks\n\nControl how poems are separated in the manuscript:\n\n| Break Style | Appearance |\n|-------------|------------|\n| **Page Break** | Each poem starts on a new page |\n| **Section Mark** | Centered mark (e.g., * * *) between poems |\n| **Double Space** | Extra blank line between poems |\n| **None** | Poems flow together continuously |\n\nFor most poetry collections, **Page Break** is the standard — each poem begins on its own page.\n\n## Poem Titles\n\nYou can control whether poem titles appear as headings in the manuscript:\n- **Include File Titles**: Shows each poem's title as a heading above it\n- Disable this if your poems have their own title lines in the content\n\nThis setting is in Export Options.\n\n## Export Formats\n\nPoetry manuscripts can be exported in all available formats:\n\n| Format | Best For |\n|--------|----------|\n| **PDF** | Submissions, chapbooks, reading copies — preserves exact line placement |\n| **RTF** | Editor collaboration, Word-compatible |\n| **HTML** | Online publication, web journals |\n| **Markdown** | Plain text archives, version control |\n\n### Why PDF Is Usually Best for Poetry\nPDF preserves line breaks, spacing, and indentation exactly. Other formats may reflow text depending on the reader's settings, which can alter the visual layout of a poem.\n\n### Exporting\n1. Open the Manuscript view\n2. Tap the export/share button\n3. Choose your format\n4. Configure options (page setup, back matter toggles)\n5. Save or share\n\n## Collection Export\n\nIn addition to full manuscript export, you can export individual collections:\n1. Open a **Collection**\n2. Tap the export/share button\n3. Only poems in that collection are included\n4. Same format options apply\n\nThis is useful for:\n- Submitting a specific group of poems to a journal\n- Creating themed chapbooks\n- Sharing a curated selection with beta readers\n\n## Tips\n\n### One Poem Per Page\nFor submissions and chapbooks, use **Page Break** as the section break style. Most editors and publishers expect each poem to start on its own page.\n\n### Order Matters\nArrange your poems thoughtfully before exporting. The sequence of poems in a collection or manuscript is a creative decision — it shapes how readers experience your work.\n\n### Exclude Experiments\nUse the **Include in Manuscript** toggle to keep exploratory drafts in your project without cluttering the export.\n\n### Check Line Breaks in PDF\nAlways preview the PDF to ensure long lines wrap as intended. Adjust page margins if lines are breaking awkwardly.\n\n## See Also\n- [Poetry Mode Overview](61-poetry-mode-overview.md)\n- [Manuscript Structure](../3-projects/36-manuscript-structure.md)\n- [Export Options](../9-publishing/91-export-options.md)\n- [PDF Export](../9-publishing/92-pdf-export.md)\n\n---\n",
              "createdDate": "2026-02-11T12:00:00Z",
